  "it_is_a_diagnostic_not_a_verdict": "G004 measured greedy divergence ranking candidates OPPOSITELY to capability, so a candidate that diverges early may be better. This describes HOW a candidate departs, never whether it is worse.",
  "not_delivered": "top-k overlap and logit margins. Both need per-step LOGITS and the harness exposes token ids only. First divergence, divergence rate, recovery and control-token changes come from the token streams and are delivered.",
  "commit": "1897ee227c23c258c0edc39360465f03bece67a1",
  "the_G004_law_shown_directly": "g032 diverges from G0 on 16/16 prompts, at a mean rate of 0.816 with median first divergence at token 4 -- 81.6% of positions differ. Yet G076 scored it EQUAL OR BETTER than G0 on every capability dimension and G126/G128 put both at 0.98. A candidate can rewrite four fifths of its output and be equally capable, which is exactly why G004 recorded divergence ranking candidates OPPOSITELY to capability, and why this is a diagnostic.",
  "the_control_token_signal_connects_to_truncation": "13 control-token mismatches across 16 prompts. g032 handles im_start/im_end differently from G0, and that is the same artifact G076 measured truncating twice as often and G100 measured needing 1.59x the tokens per task. Three instruments, one behaviour: this candidate manages its own turn boundaries differently.",
  "recovery_is_rare": "only 3 of 16 diverging prompts re-converge and stay converged. Once greedy decoding forks it mostly stays forked, which is why 'first divergence token' is the informative number and 'divergence rate' is largely a function of where the fork happened."
